/* ==========================================================================
   Wild Wisdom Global Challenge 2026-27 — "From Coastline to the Seafloor"
   Mock Exam Question Bank
   Chapters:
     1. Marine Diversity and Distribution
     2. Ocean Ecosystems, Habitats and Interdependence
     3. Life Functions Underwater (Anatomy & Life Processes)
     4. Adaptations and Behaviour
     5. Challenges to Ocean Health
     6. Human-Ocean Interactions and Conservation Stewardship
   ========================================================================== */

const QUESTION_BANK = [

// ============================== CHAPTER 1 ==================================
{id:"c1-01", chapter:1, q:"Scientists describe biodiversity at three levels. Which of the following is NOT one of them?", options:["Genetic diversity","Species diversity","Ecosystem diversity","Population density"], correct:3, explain:"The three recognised levels of biodiversity are genetic, species and ecosystem diversity. Population density is a related but separate ecological measurement."},
{id:"c1-02", chapter:1, q:"An ecosystem has 10 species, with individuals spread almost equally among them. Another ecosystem also has 10 species, but 95% of organisms belong to just one species. Both have the same species richness, but differ in:", options:["Species evenness","Genetic diversity","Ecosystem diversity","Endemism"], correct:0, explain:"Species evenness describes how evenly individuals are distributed among species — richness alone (the number of species) doesn't capture this."},
{id:"c1-03", chapter:1, q:"Why do scientists say 'species richness is not the same as biodiversity'?", options:["Because biodiversity also includes genetic and ecosystem-level variation, not just species counts","Because richness only applies to land animals","Because biodiversity ignores species counts entirely","Because richness is always higher in the ocean"], correct:0, explain:"Biodiversity is broader than just counting species — it includes genetic diversity within species and the diversity of ecosystems too."},
{id:"c1-04", chapter:1, q:"Which group of marine organisms includes the vast majority of marine biodiversity, despite being less familiar to most people?", options:["Vertebrates","Invertebrates","Marine mammals","Seabirds"], correct:1, explain:"Although whales, fish and turtles are more familiar, invertebrates (like corals, molluscs, crustaceans and jellyfish) make up most marine biodiversity."},
{id:"c1-05", chapter:1, q:"Sharks and rays belong to which group of fish, distinguished by a skeleton made mainly of cartilage rather than bone?", options:["Osteichthyes (bony fish)","Chondrichthyes (cartilaginous fish)","Cephalopods","Echinoderms"], correct:1, explain:"Chondrichthyes (sharks, rays, skates and chimaeras) have skeletons made of cartilage, which is lighter and more flexible than bone."},
{id:"c1-06", chapter:1, q:"Which statement about whales is correct?", options:["Whales are fish because they live in the ocean","Whales are mammals that breathe air using lungs","Whales extract oxygen from water using gills","Whales are a type of large fish related to sharks"], correct:1, explain:"Whales are marine mammals — they breathe air with lungs, are warm-blooded, and nurse their young with milk, unlike fish which use gills."},
{id:"c1-07", chapter:1, q:"Which of these is an example of a marine reptile?", options:["Dolphin","Sea turtle","Penguin","Tuna"], correct:1, explain:"Sea turtles (along with sea snakes and marine iguanas) are marine reptiles — they breathe air but spend much of their lives in the ocean."},
{id:"c1-08", chapter:1, q:"Corals belong to which animal group, along with jellyfish and sea anemones?", options:["Molluscs","Echinoderms","Cnidarians","Crustaceans"], correct:2, explain:"Corals are cnidarians — animals with specialised stinging cells — the same broad group as jellyfish and sea anemones."},
{id:"c1-09", chapter:1, q:"Octopus and squid, known for their intelligence and rapid colour-changing skin, belong to which group?", options:["Cephalopods (a type of mollusc)","Crustaceans","Echinoderms","Cnidarians"], correct:0, explain:"Octopus and squid are cephalopods, a highly intelligent group within the molluscs."},
{id:"c1-10", chapter:1, q:"Sea stars, sea urchins and sea cucumbers, many of which show radial symmetry and can regenerate lost body parts, belong to which group?", options:["Cnidarians","Echinoderms","Cephalopods","Crustaceans"], correct:1, explain:"Echinoderms include sea stars, sea urchins and sea cucumbers — many show radial symmetry and strong regenerative abilities."},
{id:"c1-11", chapter:1, q:"What is plankton primarily defined by?", options:["Their evolutionary relationships","Their lifestyle of drifting with ocean currents","Their large body size","Their ability to breathe air"], correct:1, explain:"Plankton are defined by lifestyle, not evolutionary relationship — they drift with currents rather than actively swimming against them."},
{id:"c1-12", chapter:1, q:"Which type of plankton photosynthesises and forms the base of many marine food webs?", options:["Zooplankton","Phytoplankton","Krill","Nekton"], correct:1, explain:"Phytoplankton are microscopic organisms that photosynthesise like plants, forming the foundation of most marine food webs and producing much of the world's oxygen."},
{id:"c1-13", chapter:1, q:"Which factor is described as one of the most powerful drivers of where marine species are found, affecting growth, reproduction and metabolism?", options:["Temperature","Colour of the water","Number of predators only","Tide timing"], correct:0, explain:"Temperature strongly influences species distribution because most organisms can only survive within a specific temperature range."},
{id:"c1-14", chapter:1, q:"The ocean zone that receives enough sunlight for photosynthesis is called the:", options:["Midnight zone","Twilight zone","Sunlit (photic) zone","Abyssal zone"], correct:2, explain:"The sunlit or photic zone extends from the surface to where enough light penetrates for phytoplankton to photosynthesise."},
{id:"c1-15", chapter:1, q:"What is 'upwelling'?", options:["The sinking of warm surface water","The upward movement of deep, nutrient-rich water toward the surface","The rising of sea levels due to climate change","A type of coral bleaching"], correct:1, explain:"Upwelling brings nutrient-rich deep water to the surface, boosting phytoplankton growth and making these regions highly productive fisheries."},
{id:"c1-16", chapter:1, q:"The pattern where species richness tends to increase toward the tropics and decrease toward the poles is called the:", options:["Latitudinal biodiversity gradient","Coral Triangle effect","Ecological niche pattern","Upwelling gradient"], correct:0, explain:"This well-documented pattern, the latitudinal biodiversity gradient, is linked to greater solar energy, longer evolutionary stability and higher productivity in tropical regions."},
{id:"c1-17", chapter:1, q:"An 'ecological niche' refers to:", options:["The exact GPS location of a species","The role a species plays in its ecosystem, including what it eats and how it interacts with others","A type of coral reef","The genetic makeup of a species"], correct:1, explain:"A niche describes a species' role within its ecosystem — what it eats, where it lives, and how it interacts with other organisms — allowing many species to coexist without excessive competition."},
{id:"c1-18", chapter:1, q:"Which of these marine regions is known as the 'Coral Triangle' and is considered the global centre of marine biodiversity?", options:["A region in the Indo-Pacific including Indonesia, Malaysia and the Philippines","The Mariana Trench","The Gulf of Mannar only","The Arctic Ocean"], correct:0, explain:"The Coral Triangle spans parts of Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Papua New Guinea, the Solomon Islands and Timor-Leste, and contains roughly 76% of known coral species."},
{id:"c1-19", chapter:1, q:"Which Indian marine region, located between India and Sri Lanka, is known for coral reefs, seagrass meadows and dugongs?", options:["Gulf of Mannar","Gulf of Kachchh","Andaman Sea","Bay of Bengal"], correct:0, explain:"The Gulf of Mannar, between India and Sri Lanka, supports coral reefs, seagrass meadows, mangroves, dugongs, sea cucumbers and reef fish."},
{id:"c1-20", chapter:1, q:"What is an 'endemic species'?", options:["A species found only in one geographic region and nowhere else","A species that has gone extinct","Any species living in the ocean","A species that migrates across oceans"], correct:0, explain:"Endemic species occur naturally in only one region, often because of long-term geographic isolation, which also makes them especially vulnerable to habitat loss."},
{id:"c1-21", chapter:1, q:"On the IUCN Red List, which category indicates the highest extinction risk (short of being extinct in the wild)?", options:["Near Threatened","Vulnerable","Critically Endangered","Least Concern"], correct:2, explain:"The IUCN Red List categories run from Least Concern up through Near Threatened, Vulnerable, Endangered, and Critically Endangered (the highest risk before Extinct in the Wild/Extinct)."},
{id:"c1-22", chapter:1, q:"What is 'bycatch'?", options:["The main species a fishery intends to catch","The accidental capture of non-target species during fishing","A method of tagging fish for research","A term for overfished species"], correct:1, explain:"Bycatch is the accidental capture of species that fishers did not intend to catch, such as sea turtles, dolphins and seabirds."},
{id:"c1-23", chapter:1, q:"Robotic underwater vehicles controlled remotely from a ship, used to explore depths too dangerous for human divers, are called:", options:["AUVs only","ROVs (Remotely Operated Vehicles)","Sonars","Submersible whales"], correct:1, explain:"ROVs are robotic vehicles controlled from the surface, carrying cameras and robotic arms to explore deep and dangerous ocean environments."},
{id:"c1-24", chapter:1, q:"Scientists can detect which species are present in an ecosystem by analysing genetic traces left in water samples, such as skin cells or mucus. This technique is called:", options:["Sonar mapping","Environmental DNA (eDNA)","Satellite telemetry","Taxonomy"], correct:1, explain:"eDNA analysis allows scientists to detect species from genetic material in water samples, without needing to directly observe or capture the organism."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 2 ==================================
{id:"c2-01", chapter:2, q:"What is the difference between a 'habitat' and an 'ecosystem'?", options:["They mean exactly the same thing","A habitat is where an organism lives; an ecosystem includes organisms, physical conditions and their interactions","An ecosystem is smaller than a habitat","A habitat only exists on land"], correct:1, explain:"A habitat is simply the place an organism lives (e.g. a mangrove root), while an ecosystem includes the organisms, the physical environment, and the interactions between them."},
{id:"c2-02", chapter:2, q:"Which of the following are 'abiotic factors' in a marine ecosystem?", options:["Fish and coral","Temperature, sunlight and currents","Bacteria and plankton","Predators and prey"], correct:1, explain:"Abiotic factors are non-living environmental conditions such as temperature, sunlight, currents and nutrients — as opposed to biotic (living) factors."},
{id:"c2-03", chapter:2, q:"Why are coral reefs often called the 'rainforests of the sea'?", options:["Because they contain actual rainforest trees","Because they occupy less than 1% of the seafloor yet support about 25% of ocean biodiversity","Because they only exist near tropical rainforests","Because they receive the most rainfall"], correct:1, explain:"Despite covering a tiny fraction of the seafloor, coral reefs' complex 3D structures support an extraordinary share of marine biodiversity."},
{id:"c2-04", chapter:2, q:"What is the symbiotic relationship between corals and zooxanthellae?", options:["The algae eat the coral's tissue","The algae photosynthesise and provide energy to the coral, while the coral provides shelter and nutrients","Zooxanthellae are parasites that harm corals","There is no real relationship between them"], correct:1, explain:"This mutualistic relationship is central to reef productivity — corals rely heavily on the energy their zooxanthellae produce through photosynthesis."},
{id:"c2-05", chapter:2, q:"What happens during 'coral bleaching'?", options:["Corals grow faster in cold water","Corals expel their zooxanthellae under heat stress, turning pale or white","Corals change colour to attract mates","Coral reefs are painted white by scientists for tracking"], correct:1, explain:"When ocean temperatures rise beyond normal ranges, corals may expel their symbiotic algae, causing bleaching; if stress continues, corals can die."},
{id:"c2-06", chapter:2, q:"Which specialised root structures help some mangrove species obtain oxygen in waterlogged, low-oxygen mud?", options:["Pneumatophores","Chromatophores","Photophores","Rhizomes"], correct:0, explain:"Pneumatophores are aerial roots that extend above the mud, helping mangroves access oxygen in oxygen-poor soils."},
{id:"c2-07", chapter:2, q:"Why are mangroves, seagrasses and salt marshes called 'blue carbon' ecosystems?", options:["Because they turn the ocean blue","Because they store large amounts of carbon in their biomass and sediments","Because they only exist in blue-coloured water","Because they absorb blue light specifically"], correct:1, explain:"These coastal ecosystems store carbon very efficiently in plant tissue and sediment, making them important for climate regulation."},
{id:"c2-08", chapter:2, q:"Seagrasses are different from seaweeds because:", options:["Seagrasses are true flowering plants with roots and vascular tissue, while seaweeds are algae","Seaweeds have flowers and seagrasses do not","Seagrasses only grow in the deep sea","There is no real difference"], correct:0, explain:"Seagrasses evolved from land plants and have roots, leaves, vascular tissue, flowers and seeds — unlike seaweeds, which are simpler algae."},
{id:"c2-09", chapter:2, q:"Dugongs are sometimes called 'sea cows' because they primarily feed on:", options:["Fish","Seagrass","Coral","Plankton"], correct:1, explain:"Dugongs graze on seagrass meadows, making healthy seagrass ecosystems essential for their survival."},
{id:"c2-10", chapter:2, q:"What is the 'pelagic zone'?", options:["The seafloor only","The water column away from coastlines and the seafloor","A type of coral reef","A polar-only region"], correct:1, explain:"The pelagic (open ocean) zone refers to the water column away from coasts and the seafloor, and forms the largest habitat on Earth."},
{id:"c2-11", chapter:2, q:"Organisms like tuna, sharks and squid that actively swim against ocean currents are called:", options:["Plankton","Nekton","Benthos","Detritivores"], correct:1, explain:"Nekton are organisms capable of powerful, directed swimming, unlike plankton which drift with currents."},
{id:"c2-12", chapter:2, q:"What is 'marine snow'?", options:["Frozen sea spray in polar regions","Sinking organic material such as dead plankton and waste that feeds deep-sea ecosystems","A type of white coral","Snowfall that lands directly on the ocean"], correct:1, explain:"Marine snow transports nutrients and energy from productive surface waters down into the deep sea, where food is otherwise scarce."},
{id:"c2-13", chapter:2, q:"How do hydrothermal vent ecosystems obtain energy, given there is no sunlight at those depths?", options:["Bacteria use chemosynthesis, converting chemicals like hydrogen sulfide into energy","They rely entirely on marine snow from the surface","They use bioluminescence to photosynthesise","They don't need energy at all"], correct:0, explain:"Vent bacteria use chemosynthesis instead of photosynthesis, forming the foundation of food webs that support tube worms, vent crabs and shrimp."},
{id:"c2-14", chapter:2, q:"What is a 'trophic level'?", options:["A depth measurement in the ocean","A feeding position within a food web (e.g. producer, primary consumer, apex predator)","A type of ocean current","A category of coral"], correct:1, explain:"Trophic levels describe an organism's position in a food chain — from producers like phytoplankton up to apex predators like sharks."},
{id:"c2-15", chapter:2, q:"Why do ecosystems typically contain many plankton but very few apex predators?", options:["Apex predators reproduce faster than plankton","Energy decreases at each step up the food web, so less energy is available to support organisms at higher trophic levels","Plankton are protected by law","Apex predators eat only plankton directly"], correct:1, explain:"Because organisms use much of their energy for movement, growth and reproduction, only a fraction of energy transfers to the next trophic level, limiting how many top predators an ecosystem can support."},
{id:"c2-16", chapter:2, q:"A 'keystone species' is one that:", options:["Is the most numerous species in an ecosystem","Has an ecological impact far greater than its abundance would suggest","Only lives in the deep sea","Cannot be removed from an ecosystem by natural causes"], correct:1, explain:"Sea otters are a classic keystone species example: by eating sea urchins, they prevent overgrazing of kelp forests — removing otters can trigger a trophic cascade."},
{id:"c2-17", chapter:2, q:"In a mutualistic relationship like that between cleaner wrasse and larger fish, what happens?", options:["Only one species benefits while the other is harmed","Both species benefit","Neither species is affected","One species is a parasite of the other"], correct:1, explain:"The cleaner wrasse gets food by removing parasites, while the larger fish benefits from reduced parasite loads — both species benefit."},
{id:"c2-18", chapter:2, q:"Which of these is an example of commensalism, where one species benefits and the other is largely unaffected?", options:["Corals and zooxanthellae","Remoras attaching to sharks for transport and food scraps","Sea otters and sea urchins","Cleaner wrasse and larger fish"], correct:1, explain:"Remoras gain transportation, protection and food scraps from sharks, while the shark is usually not significantly affected either way."},
{id:"c2-19", chapter:2, q:"What are 'ecosystem services'?", options:["Fees charged for visiting marine parks","Benefits that humans obtain from ecosystems, such as food, climate regulation and coastal protection","A list of endangered species","Government regulations on fishing"], correct:1, explain:"Ecosystem services include provisioning (food, medicine), regulating (climate, coastal protection), supporting (nutrient cycling) and cultural services."},
{id:"c2-20", chapter:2, q:"Why do phytoplankton matter so much for the whole planet, not just the ocean?", options:["They are the tastiest food for whales","They contribute significantly to global oxygen production through photosynthesis","They control ocean tides","They prevent all storms"], correct:1, explain:"As microscopic photosynthesisers, phytoplankton are a major source of the planet's oxygen and form the base of most marine food webs."},
{id:"c2-21", chapter:2, q:"The Great Barrier Reef, the world's largest coral reef system, is located off the coast of:", options:["India","Australia","Mexico","South Africa"], correct:1, explain:"The Great Barrier Reef stretches over 2,300 kilometres off the coast of Australia and supports thousands of reef species."},
{id:"c2-22", chapter:2, q:"Why is the Sundarbans significant among the world's mangrove ecosystems?", options:["It is the smallest mangrove forest in the world","It is the world's largest mangrove forest system, shared by India and Bangladesh","It contains no wildlife","It is located in the Pacific Ocean"], correct:1, explain:"The Sundarbans, spanning more than 10,000 sq km across India and Bangladesh, is the largest mangrove forest system on Earth."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 3 ==================================
{id:"c3-01", chapter:3, q:"An organism that can be divided into two mirror-image halves along a single line (like most fish and sharks) shows which body plan?", options:["Radial symmetry","Bilateral symmetry","Asymmetry","Hydrostatic symmetry"], correct:1, explain:"Bilateral symmetry, seen in fish, sharks and turtles, often supports efficient directional movement for hunting or escaping predators."},
{id:"c3-02", chapter:3, q:"Jellyfish and sea anemones, which can be divided into similar sections around a central point, show which body plan?", options:["Bilateral symmetry","Radial symmetry","Endoskeleton structure","Convergent symmetry"], correct:1, explain:"Radial symmetry allows organisms like sea anemones to respond to prey or threats coming from any direction."},
{id:"c3-03", chapter:3, q:"Crabs, lobsters and shrimp have external skeletons that must be periodically shed as they grow. This is called:", options:["Metamorphosis","Moulting","Regeneration","Camouflage"], correct:1, explain:"Because exoskeletons don't grow continuously, crustaceans must moult (shed their shell) periodically to grow larger."},
{id:"c3-04", chapter:3, q:"Sharks and dolphins have similar streamlined, torpedo-shaped bodies even though one is a fish and the other is a mammal. This is an example of:", options:["Radial symmetry","Convergent evolution","Bioluminescence","Symbiosis"], correct:1, explain:"Convergent evolution occurs when unrelated species independently evolve similar traits because they face similar environmental challenges — here, moving efficiently through water."},
{id:"c3-05", chapter:3, q:"Why is breathing underwater more difficult than breathing air?", options:["Water contains no oxygen at all","Water holds much less oxygen than air and is denser, requiring more effort to extract oxygen","Fish don't actually need oxygen","Underwater breathing is easier, not harder"], correct:1, explain:"Water contains far less dissolved oxygen than air, and is heavier to move across respiratory surfaces, making gills and specialised breathing adaptations necessary."},
{id:"c3-06", chapter:3, q:"What is the main function of gills in fish?", options:["To help the fish swim faster","To extract oxygen from water as it flows across them","To store food for digestion","To produce bioluminescent light"], correct:1, explain:"Gills are respiratory organs with thin, blood-vessel-rich surfaces where oxygen from water diffuses into the bloodstream."},
{id:"c3-07", chapter:3, q:"Why must whales, dolphins and seals regularly return to the surface even though they live in water?", options:["They are actually fish and need to feed at the surface","They are mammals with lungs and must breathe air","They surface only to sleep","They need sunlight for photosynthesis"], correct:1, explain:"Marine mammals evolved from land-dwelling ancestors and retained lungs, so despite living in water, they must surface to breathe air."},
{id:"c3-08", chapter:3, q:"A whale's blowhole is best described as:", options:["A gill used to extract oxygen from water","The whale's nostril, located on top of its head","A sensory organ for detecting prey","An organ used only for making sounds"], correct:1, explain:"The blowhole is a modified nostril that lets whales breathe quickly at the surface without lifting their whole head out of the water."},
{id:"c3-09", chapter:3, q:"Filter feeders like baleen whales and whale sharks obtain food by:", options:["Actively hunting large prey with sharp teeth","Straining tiny organisms such as plankton or krill from large volumes of water","Only scavenging dead animals","Photosynthesising like plants"], correct:1, explain:"Filter feeders use specialised structures (like baleen plates) to trap small organisms while letting water pass through — useful where plankton occur in huge numbers."},
{id:"c3-10", chapter:3, q:"Parrotfish are important on coral reefs mainly because they:", options:["Hunt sharks","Graze algae off coral surfaces, helping prevent reefs from being overgrown","Produce bioluminescent light","Filter feed on plankton only"], correct:1, explain:"As grazers, parrotfish help control algal growth on reefs, which helps maintain the balance needed for corals to thrive."},
{id:"c3-11", chapter:3, q:"Sea cucumbers, which extract organic material directly from ocean sediments, are examples of:", options:["Filter feeders","Deposit feeders","Apex predators","Scavengers only"], correct:1, explain:"Deposit feeders like sea cucumbers consume sediment, digesting the organic particles and microorganisms within it."},
{id:"c3-12", chapter:3, q:"Squid and octopuses move rapidly through water by:", options:["Using fins like fish","Jet propulsion — drawing water into their body and expelling it through a funnel-like structure","Flying above the water","Rolling along the seafloor"], correct:1, explain:"Jet propulsion allows squid and octopuses fast bursts of movement for escape or hunting, though it uses more energy than steady swimming."},
{id:"c3-13", chapter:3, q:"What is the main function of a swim bladder in many bony fish?", options:["To help the fish see in the dark","To regulate buoyancy so the fish can stay at a chosen depth without constantly swimming","To produce electricity","To store extra oxygen for deep dives only"], correct:1, explain:"By adjusting the gas volume in their swim bladder, fish can rise, sink or remain suspended at a particular depth without expending constant energy."},
{id:"c3-14", chapter:3, q:"Since sharks lack swim bladders, how do many species maintain buoyancy instead?", options:["They cannot control buoyancy at all and constantly sink","Using large, oil-rich livers and lift generated while swimming","By breathing air like whales","By floating on the surface only"], correct:1, explain:"Shark livers contain oils that are less dense than water, helping improve buoyancy, along with lift generated as they swim."},
{id:"c3-15", chapter:3, q:"The sensory system in fish that detects vibrations and water movement along the sides of their body is called the:", options:["Lateral line","Ampullae of Lorenzini","Melon organ","Operculum"], correct:0, explain:"The lateral line helps fish detect nearby movement, avoid collisions, and coordinate schooling, even in murky or dark water."},
{id:"c3-16", chapter:3, q:"Dolphins locate prey in murky water by producing clicking sounds and interpreting the returning echoes. This ability is called:", options:["Electroreception","Echolocation","Bioluminescence","Chemoreception"], correct:1, explain:"Echolocation lets dolphins and toothed whales determine an object's distance, size, shape and movement by analysing returning sound echoes."},
{id:"c3-17", chapter:3, q:"Sharks and rays can detect the weak electrical fields produced by other animals' muscles and nerves using organs called:", options:["Ampullae of Lorenzini","Lateral lines","Blowholes","Chromatophores"], correct:0, explain:"Ampullae of Lorenzini are jelly-filled pores around a shark's head that detect tiny electrical signals, helping locate hidden prey."},
{id:"c3-18", chapter:3, q:"Reproduction where eggs and sperm are released into the surrounding water, and fertilisation happens outside the body, is called:", options:["Internal fertilisation","External fertilisation","Asexual reproduction only","Metamorphosis"], correct:1, explain:"External fertilisation is common in many fish, corals and sea urchins, and typically involves producing enormous numbers of eggs since many will not survive."},
{id:"c3-19", chapter:3, q:"What is 'metamorphosis' in marine organisms?", options:["A change in water temperature","A major transformation in body form as an organism develops from larva to adult","The process of moulting an exoskeleton","A type of camouflage"], correct:1, explain:"Many marine organisms undergo metamorphosis, where larvae look completely different from adults, reducing competition between life stages."},
{id:"c3-20", chapter:3, q:"In seahorses, which is true about reproduction?", options:["Females carry the developing embryos in a pouch","Males carry the developing embryos in a specialised brood pouch","Seahorses do not reproduce sexually","Seahorses lay eggs directly on the seafloor with no parental care"], correct:1, explain:"Male seahorses carry developing embryos in a brood pouch and release fully formed young — an unusual reproductive strategy among vertebrates."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 4 ==================================
{id:"c4-01", chapter:4, q:"In biology, what is the precise meaning of 'adaptation'?", options:["Something an animal learns during its own lifetime","An inherited trait, shaped over many generations by natural selection, that improves survival and reproduction","Any change an organism makes to its daily routine","A behaviour copied from another species"], correct:1, explain:"Unlike the everyday use of the word, a biological adaptation accumulates over generations through natural selection — it is not learned or chosen within one lifetime."},
{id:"c4-02", chapter:4, q:"Which three ingredients does natural selection require to work, according to the chapter?", options:["Variation, heredity, and differential survival/reproduction","Speed, size, and colour","Camouflage, migration, and bioluminescence","Warm water, sunlight, and oxygen"], correct:0, explain:"Natural selection requires variation among individuals, the ability to pass traits to offspring (heredity), and traits that affect survival and reproductive success."},
{id:"c4-03", chapter:4, q:"Giant tube worms living near hydrothermal vents survive without a mouth or digestive system because:", options:["They absorb sunlight directly","They house chemosynthetic bacteria in an organ called a trophosome, which converts vent chemicals into energy","They eat through their skin","They don't need any energy source"], correct:1, explain:"The bacteria inside the trophosome use chemosynthesis to produce nutrients that support the tube worm, an extreme adaptation to a sunless environment."},
{id:"c4-04", chapter:4, q:"Camouflage where an animal's colour and pattern closely resemble its surroundings is called:", options:["Mimicry","Background matching","Bioluminescence","Echolocation"], correct:1, explain:"Background matching is one of the two broad forms of camouflage, alongside mimicry (imitating another species)."},
{id:"c4-05", chapter:4, q:"The mimic octopus is famous for:", options:["Producing the loudest sound in the ocean","Impersonating several different dangerous species, such as lionfish or sea snakes, by changing its shape and movement","Being the only bioluminescent octopus","Having the largest eyes of any animal"], correct:1, explain:"The mimic octopus can switch between multiple disguises depending on which predator is nearby, rather than relying on one fixed appearance."},
{id:"c4-06", chapter:4, q:"Cephalopods like cuttlefish change colour almost instantly using pigment-filled skin cells called:", options:["Chromatophores","Ampullae of Lorenzini","Pneumatophores","Photophores"], correct:0, explain:"Chromatophores are controlled directly by the nervous system, letting cephalopods shift colour and pattern within a fraction of a second."},
{id:"c4-07", chapter:4, q:"Roughly what percentage of animals living between 200 and 1,000 metres depth are estimated to produce their own light?", options:["About 5%","About 30%","About 80%","Nearly 100%"], correct:2, explain:"According to NOAA Ocean Exploration, roughly 80% of animals in this deep mid-water zone are bioluminescent, making it possibly the most common form of communication on the planet."},
{id:"c4-08", chapter:4, q:"Bioluminescence is produced through a chemical reaction between:", options:["Water and salt","Luciferin, the enzyme luciferase, and oxygen","Carbon dioxide and sunlight","Melanin and heat"], correct:1, explain:"This reaction releases light with almost none of the energy lost as heat, unlike an ordinary light bulb."},
{id:"c4-09", chapter:4, q:"When threatened, a sea cucumber may eject some of its own internal organs. This defensive strategy is called:", options:["Metamorphosis","Evisceration","Bioluminescence","Chemosynthesis"], correct:1, explain:"Sea cucumbers can eject organs as a decoy or to release sticky toxic threads, then regrow the lost tissue within weeks."},
{id:"c4-10", chapter:4, q:"A starfish deliberately detaching its own arm to escape a predator is called:", options:["Camouflage","Autotomy","Migration","Countershading"], correct:1, explain:"Autotomy is a voluntary, deliberate escape strategy, triggered by a specific neurohormone, rather than an accidental loss."},
{id:"c4-11", chapter:4, q:"Why is sound often a more effective way to communicate underwater than sight?", options:["Sound cannot travel through water at all","Sound travels roughly four times faster in water than in air and can carry across huge distances","Light travels faster underwater than sound","Underwater animals cannot hear sound"], correct:1, explain:"Because sound travels efficiently and quickly through water, many marine mammals rely on it as their primary way to communicate and navigate."},
{id:"c4-12", chapter:4, q:"Which whale group (including humpback and blue whales) produces low-frequency pulses, moans and whoops that travel very long distances?", options:["Odontocetes (toothed whales)","Mysticetes (baleen whales)","Pinnipeds","Cephalopods"], correct:1, explain:"Mysticetes, or baleen whales, produce low-frequency sounds, while odontocetes (toothed whales like orcas and dolphins) produce higher-frequency clicks and whistles."},
{id:"c4-13", chapter:4, q:"What is the key difference between 'shoaling' and 'schooling' in fish?", options:["They mean exactly the same thing","Shoaling is any loose social gathering; schooling is a tightly coordinated, synchronised group of the same species","Schooling only happens in freshwater","Shoaling only happens among predators"], correct:1, explain:"True schooling — tight synchrony in speed and direction — is seen in only about a quarter of fish species, while shoaling (looser social gathering) is more common."},
{id:"c4-14", chapter:4, q:"Which of these is NOT a commonly cited benefit of schooling behaviour?", options:["Confusing predators (confusion effect)","Reducing individual predation risk (dilution effect)","Improving foraging efficiency","Guaranteeing that no individual is ever eaten"], correct:3, explain:"Schooling reduces risk through confusion and dilution effects and can improve feeding, but it does not guarantee complete safety for every individual."},
{id:"c4-15", chapter:4, q:"Migration in marine animals is usually driven by the need to:", options:["Avoid all contact with other members of their species","Find food, reach breeding grounds, or avoid unfavourable conditions","Stay in exactly the same spot their whole lives","Change their body colour permanently"], correct:1, explain:"Whether it's whales, turtles or tuna, migration is generally linked to feeding, breeding, or escaping conditions like extreme cold."},
{id:"c4-16", chapter:4, q:"The sea turtle 'lost years' refers to:", options:["The years turtles spend in captivity","The poorly understood period between when hatchlings leave the nesting beach and when they reappear as juveniles at feeding grounds","The years before a turtle's egg hatches","A period when turtles stop growing"], correct:1, explain:"Satellite tagging research helped reveal that young turtles actively use areas like the Sargasso Sea during this period, rather than simply drifting passively."},
{id:"c4-17", chapter:4, q:"The sense that allows sea turtles to detect Earth's magnetic field and helps guide their long migrations is called:", options:["Echolocation","Magnetoreception","Electroreception","Chemoreception"], correct:1, explain:"Magnetoreception lets turtles sense the unique magnetic 'signature' of coastal locations, helping them navigate back to their natal beach even after journeys of thousands of kilometres."},
{id:"c4-18", chapter:4, q:"What is 'natal homing' in sea turtles?", options:["The tendency of adult turtles to return to nest on the same beach where they themselves hatched","A turtle's ability to build a nest anywhere","A turtle's first migration as a hatchling","A method scientists use to tag turtles"], correct:0, explain:"Natal homing means adult turtles often return to their birth beach to nest, sometimes after journeys spanning thousands of kilometres and many years."},
{id:"c4-19", chapter:4, q:"Which structural feature helps reduce drag and allows fast swimmers like tuna and dolphins to move efficiently through water?", options:["A streamlined, tapered body shape","A hard exoskeleton","Radial symmetry","A hydrostatic skeleton"], correct:0, explain:"Streamlined bodies reduce resistance (drag) in water, which is much denser than air, allowing efficient, energy-saving movement."},
{id:"c4-20", chapter:4, q:"Counter-illumination, a bioluminescent camouflage strategy used by some marine animals, works by:", options:["Making the animal glow brightly to scare predators away","Matching faint light from above on the underside of the body, making the animal harder to see in silhouette from below","Producing loud sounds instead of light","Turning the animal's entire body transparent"], correct:1, explain:"Counter-illumination helps an animal blend into the faint light coming from the surface above, making it less visible to predators looking upward from below."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 5 ==================================
{id:"c5-01", chapter:5, q:"Why do oceans play an important role in slowing down global warming on land?", options:["Oceans reflect all sunlight back into space","Oceans absorb large amounts of heat and carbon dioxide from the atmosphere","Oceans have stopped absorbing any heat","Oceans are unaffected by the atmosphere"], correct:1, explain:"By absorbing excess heat and CO2, oceans reduce warming on land to some extent — but this creates major changes within marine ecosystems themselves."},
{id:"c5-02", chapter:5, q:"What is 'ocean acidification'?", options:["Oceans becoming more acidic as they absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere","A type of pollution caused only by oil spills","The process of seawater freezing","A method used to clean plastic from the ocean"], correct:0, explain:"As CO2 dissolves into seawater, oceans become slightly more acidic, which can affect organisms that build shells or skeletons from calcium carbonate, like corals and oysters."},
{id:"c5-03", chapter:5, q:"Which of these is NOT a major cause of rising sea levels?", options:["Thermal expansion of warming seawater","Melting glaciers and ice sheets","Coral bleaching","Both A and B are causes, so C is the odd one out"], correct:3, explain:"Sea-level rise is driven by seawater expanding as it warms and by melting ice adding more water to the ocean — coral bleaching is a separate impact of warming, not a direct cause of sea-level rise."},
{id:"c5-04", chapter:5, q:"Tiny plastic fragments, formed when larger plastic breaks down in the ocean, are called:", options:["Ghost gear","Microplastics","Marine snow","Ballast water"], correct:1, explain:"Microplastics have now been detected across nearly all major ocean ecosystems, including deep oceans and polar regions."},
{id:"c5-05", chapter:5, q:"Lost or abandoned fishing gear that continues trapping fish, turtles and other marine animals is known as:", options:["Ghost gear","Bycatch","Ballast water","Marine debris only"], correct:0, explain:"Ghost gear is considered one of the major forms of marine debris, as it can keep catching animals long after being discarded."},
{id:"c5-06", chapter:5, q:"What is the difference between 'habitat loss' and 'habitat degradation'?", options:["They mean the same thing","Habitat loss is complete removal of a habitat; degradation means the habitat remains but functions less effectively","Habitat degradation only happens on land","Habitat loss cannot be caused by humans"], correct:1, explain:"For example, clearing a mangrove forest entirely is habitat loss, while pollution making a mangrove less suitable for wildlife (without removing it) is degradation."},
{id:"c5-07", chapter:5, q:"What does 'habitat fragmentation' mean?", options:["Habitats becoming larger and more connected","Breaking habitats into smaller, disconnected pieces","A habitat disappearing completely","Habitats being restored to full health"], correct:1, explain:"Even when some habitat remains, fragmentation can reduce breeding opportunities, limit movement and increase vulnerability to disturbance."},
{id:"c5-08", chapter:5, q:"Overfishing occurs when:", options:["Fish are harvested slower than they can reproduce","Fish are removed from a population faster than they can naturally replace themselves","No fish are caught at all","Fisheries use only sustainable methods"], correct:1, explain:"Like a bank account being drawn down faster than it's refilled, overfishing gradually depletes a population, sometimes without being noticed until it's severe."},
{id:"c5-09", chapter:5, q:"Why are sharks and rays especially vulnerable to overfishing compared to many bony fish?", options:["They reproduce very quickly and in huge numbers","They generally grow slowly, mature late, and produce relatively few offspring","They live only in freshwater","They are immune to fishing gear"], correct:1, explain:"Slow growth and low reproduction rates mean shark and ray populations take much longer to recover once they decline."},
{id:"c5-10", chapter:5, q:"Turtle Excluder Devices (TEDs) are used in fishing nets to:", options:["Catch more turtles for research","Allow sea turtles to escape trawl nets while retaining the target catch","Attract more fish into the net","Track fish using satellites"], correct:1, explain:"TEDs are considered one of the most successful technologies for reducing sea turtle bycatch in fisheries like shrimp trawling."},
{id:"c5-11", chapter:5, q:"What does 'IUU fishing' stand for?", options:["Illegal, Unreported and Unregulated fishing","International Union for Underwater fishing","Illegal Use of Underwater equipment","Individual, Unlicensed and Unsafe fishing"], correct:0, explain:"IUU fishing undermines sustainable fisheries management and, according to the FAO, costs the global economy billions of dollars annually."},
{id:"c5-12", chapter:5, q:"An 'invasive species' is best defined as:", options:["Any species that lives in the ocean","A species introduced outside its natural range that spreads rapidly and causes ecological, economic or social harm","A species that has gone extinct","A species that only lives in polar regions"], correct:1, explain:"Not every introduced species becomes invasive — but those that thrive without natural predators can alter food webs and damage habitats, like the lionfish invasion in the Atlantic and Caribbean."},
{id:"c5-13", chapter:5, q:"Ballast water, taken on by large ships for stability, is a major pathway for invasive species because:", options:["It kills all marine organisms instantly","It can carry plankton, larvae and small organisms that get released far from their native range","It is always sterilised before release","It only carries seaweed"], correct:1, explain:"Ships take in seawater (and its organisms) in one region and may release it thousands of kilometres away, introducing species to new ecosystems."},
{id:"c5-14", chapter:5, q:"What is the difference between 'extinction' and 'extirpation'?", options:["They are identical terms","Extinction means a species disappears everywhere; extirpation means it disappears from one area but survives elsewhere","Extirpation is always caused by humans, extinction never is","Extinction can be reversed, extirpation cannot"], correct:1, explain:"Understanding this difference helps conservationists judge where recovery efforts might still succeed — an extirpated species could potentially be reintroduced from elsewhere."},
{id:"c5-15", chapter:5, q:"What is 'functional extinction'?", options:["When a species' population declines so much it can no longer perform its ecological role, even though a few individuals remain","The complete disappearance of a species from Earth","A species being renamed by scientists","When a species is moved to a zoo"], correct:0, explain:"For example, a predator may still exist in small numbers but no longer regulate prey populations effectively — this can damage ecosystem health even before full extinction."},
{id:"c5-16", chapter:5, q:"India's dugong populations are found in regions including the Gulf of Mannar, Palk Bay, and:", options:["The Mariana Trench","The Andaman and Nicobar Islands","The Arctic Ocean","The Sargasso Sea"], correct:1, explain:"India's dugong populations depend on seagrass habitats in these regions, and are vulnerable to habitat degradation and accidental capture in fishing gear."},
{id:"c5-17", chapter:5, q:"Nearly what percentage of the world's population lives within 100 kilometres of a coastline?", options:["About 5%","About 40%","About 90%","100%"], correct:1, explain:"This concentration of people near coasts means decisions made along coastlines can affect both human communities and marine ecosystems on a massive scale."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 6 ==================================
{id:"c6-01", chapter:6, q:"What does 'Traditional Ecological Knowledge' (TEK) refer to?", options:["Knowledge only found in modern science textbooks","Environmental understanding developed by communities over generations of observing tides, seasons, and local ecosystems","A GPS-based mapping system","A rule created by international law"], correct:1, explain:"Many coastal and Indigenous communities developed detailed ecological knowledge long before modern instruments existed, and this knowledge increasingly informs conservation efforts."},
{id:"c6-02", chapter:6, q:"The Haenyeo of Jeju Island, South Korea, are known for:", options:["Building large fishing trawlers","Diving without scuba equipment to harvest shellfish and seaweed using breath-holding skills","Farming seaweed only using drones","Leading international ocean treaties"], correct:1, explain:"UNESCO has recognised Haenyeo culture as an Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, showing how humans can sustainably harvest marine resources through traditional skill."},
{id:"c6-03", chapter:6, q:"What is a 'wild capture fishery'?", options:["A fishery where organisms are farmed in tanks","Harvesting fish and other organisms directly from natural ecosystems rather than farming them","A fishery that only operates illegally","A type of aquarium"], correct:1, explain:"Most marine fisheries are wild capture fisheries, harvesting organisms directly from oceans, rivers or estuaries."},
{id:"c6-04", chapter:6, q:"Which fishing method involves dragging large nets through the water or across the seafloor?", options:["Longline fishing","Trawling","Purse seining only","Hand-lining"], correct:1, explain:"Trawling is one of several fishing methods, each with different environmental impacts and levels of bycatch."},
{id:"c6-05", chapter:6, q:"What is a Marine Protected Area (MPA)?", options:["A region where all human activity is completely banned forever","An ocean region where activities like fishing are restricted or carefully managed to protect ecosystems and biodiversity","A private beach resort","An area only used for shipping"], correct:1, explain:"MPAs may help fish populations recover by protecting important habitats such as coral reefs, mangroves and seagrass beds, and can benefit nearby fisheries too."},
{id:"c6-06", chapter:6, q:"What does 'ecosystem-based fisheries management' consider, compared to traditional single-species management?", options:["Only the price of fish in the market","Habitats, food webs, biodiversity and environmental change together, not just one species alone","Only the size of fishing boats","Nothing related to ecosystems"], correct:1, explain:"This more holistic approach is becoming increasingly important as climate change alters marine ecosystems in complex ways."},
{id:"c6-07", chapter:6, q:"The United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), often called the 'Constitution of the Oceans', was adopted in:", options:["1945","1982","2001","2023"], correct:1, explain:"UNCLOS, adopted in 1982, established frameworks including Exclusive Economic Zones (EEZs) that still guide ocean governance today."},
{id:"c6-08", chapter:6, q:"An Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) generally extends how far from a coastal country's coastline?", options:["12 nautical miles","50 nautical miles","200 nautical miles","1,000 nautical miles"], correct:2, explain:"Under UNCLOS, coastal countries generally have special rights to marine resources within 200 nautical miles of their coastline — India's EEZ covers more than 2 million square kilometres."},
{id:"c6-09", chapter:6, q:"What are the 'High Seas'?", options:["Areas within a country's territorial waters","Waters lying outside any country's Exclusive Economic Zone, accounting for nearly two-thirds of the ocean","Only the surface layer of the ocean","A term for polar oceans only"], correct:1, explain:"Because no single country manages the High Seas, protecting biodiversity there has historically been challenging — leading to newer agreements like the 2023 BBNJ (High Seas) Treaty."},
{id:"c6-10", chapter:6, q:"The Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), adopted in 1992, aims to:", options:["Only regulate international shipping routes","Conserve biodiversity, promote sustainable use of biological resources, and ensure fair sharing of benefits from genetic resources","Ban all fishing worldwide","Only protect forest ecosystems"], correct:1, explain:"Although often associated with forests, the CBD also plays an important role in marine conservation and biodiversity targets."},
{id:"c6-11", chapter:6, q:"What is 'citizen science'?", options:["Science that can only be done by professional scientists","Public participation in scientific research, such as reporting whale sightings or monitoring coral bleaching","A government policy about fishing licences","A type of underwater robot"], correct:1, explain:"Citizen science allows members of the public to contribute valuable observations that would be difficult for scientists to collect alone across huge areas."},
{id:"c6-12", chapter:6, q:"Robotic vehicles like ROVs and AUVs are useful in ocean science mainly because they:", options:["Can travel through deep, high-pressure ocean environments that are dangerous or impossible for human divers","Can only operate in shallow swimming pools","Replace the need for any scientific research","Cannot carry cameras or sensors"], correct:0, explain:"These vehicles let scientists study extreme environments like hydrothermal vents and deep trenches without risking human divers."},
{id:"c6-13", chapter:6, q:"Underwater microphones used to record whale calls and other ocean sounds are called:", options:["Sonars","Hydrophones","Chromatophores","Photophores"], correct:1, explain:"This field of study is called bioacoustics, and it helps scientists study whale migration, communication and behaviour without needing to see the animals directly."},
{id:"c6-14", chapter:6, q:"Why did humpback whale populations recover so strongly after being reduced by more than 90% during the industrial whaling era?", options:["Whales adapted to survive without any protection","International agreements restricted commercial whaling and reduced threats, allowing populations to recover over decades","Humpback whales moved permanently to freshwater rivers","No conservation action was actually needed"], correct:1, explain:"The humpback whale's recovery is often cited as one of the world's most successful marine conservation stories, showing that reducing threats allows species to rebound."},
{id:"c6-15", chapter:6, q:"How did community involvement help conserve whale sharks along India's Gujarat coast?", options:["Fishers were fined heavily with no other support","Awareness campaigns and compensation programmes helped fishers move from hunting whale sharks to protecting them","The government banned all fishing in Gujarat entirely","Whale sharks were relocated to zoos"], correct:1, explain:"Legal protection under India's Wildlife (Protection) Act, combined with community awareness and support, turned former hunters into some of the whale shark's strongest protectors."},
{id:"c6-16", chapter:6, q:"The Gahirmatha and Rushikulya coasts in Odisha are globally significant because they host mass nesting events, called arribadas, of which species?", options:["Humpback whales","Olive Ridley sea turtles","Whale sharks","Dugongs"], correct:1, explain:"Hundreds of thousands of Olive Ridley turtles come ashore over just a few nights during an arribada, making Odisha one of the most important sea turtle conservation regions in the world."},
{id:"c6-17", chapter:6, q:"The UN declared 2021-2030 as the:", options:["Decade of Ocean Science for Sustainable Development","Decade of Deep-Sea Mining","International Year of the Whale","Decade of Coral Restoration Only"], correct:0, explain:"Known as 'the Ocean Decade', this initiative aims to bring together scientists, governments, communities and organisations to support sustainable ocean management."},
{id:"c6-18", chapter:6, q:"Which of these is an example of a genuine, evidence-based way a student can contribute to ocean conservation, according to the learning material?", options:["Only professional marine biologists can help oceans","Reducing plastic waste, supporting sustainable seafood choices, and participating in citizen science projects","Ignoring ocean issues until adulthood","Avoiding all learning about marine ecosystems"], correct:1, explain:"The material emphasises that protecting oceans is a shared responsibility, and that everyday actions and citizen participation genuinely matter alongside scientific and government efforts."},


// ============================== CHAPTER 1 ADDITIONS ==================================
{id:"c1-25", chapter:1, q:"Sponges (Phylum Porifera) are unusual among animals because they:", options:["Have complex brains but no digestive system", "Lack true tissues and organs despite sometimes growing to large sizes", "Are actually a type of algae, not an animal", "Can only survive in freshwater"], correct:1, explain:"Sponges are among the simplest multicellular animals - they have specialised cells but lack true tissues and organs, which is remarkable given some species grow quite large."},
{id:"c1-26", chapter:1, q:"In a nautilus, squid or other shelled mollusc, which body structure is responsible for secreting the shell?", options:["The mantle", "The siphon", "The radula", "The operculum"], correct:0, explain:"The mantle is a sheet of tissue that secretes calcium carbonate to build and repair the shell, and also lines the body cavity housing the gills and organs."},
{id:"c1-27", chapter:1, q:"Sawfish get their common name from:", options:["Their habit of sawing through coral to find food", "A long, flat, tooth-lined snout (rostrum) resembling a saw, used to sense and strike prey", "A saw-shaped dorsal fin", "Their raspy, saw-like call"], correct:1, explain:"The sawfish's elongated, blade-like rostrum is lined with sharp teeth and is used both to detect prey via electroreception and to slash through schools of fish."},
{id:"c1-28", chapter:1, q:"Guitarfish and wedgefish - flattened, ray-like fish with shark-like tails - face a higher global extinction risk than almost any other marine fish family mainly because:", options:["They only live in freshwater lakes", "Their fins are highly valued in international trade, driving intense targeted and incidental fishing pressure", "They are immune to habitat loss", "They reproduce faster than any other fish"], correct:1, explain:"Guitarfish and wedgefish are considered among the most threatened marine fish families worldwide, largely due to demand for their fins in the shark fin trade."},
{id:"c1-29", chapter:1, q:"The eerie blue glow sometimes seen in waves and surf along coastlines at night is most commonly produced by:", options:["Dinoflagellates, a type of bioluminescent plankton", "Jellyfish stings", "Reflected moonlight only", "Dissolved salt crystals"], correct:0, explain:"Certain dinoflagellate species produce light when disturbed by wave motion or movement, creating a glowing blue effect in the water, sometimes called a 'blue tide'."},
{id:"c1-30", chapter:1, q:"Pteropods, nicknamed 'sea butterflies' because of the wing-like structures they use to swim, are a group of:", options:["Tiny swimming sea snails (molluscs) with thin shells", "Jellyfish", "Crustaceans related to krill", "Deep-sea fish"], correct:0, explain:"Pteropods are shelled, swimming molluscs that drift and swim using wing-like extensions of their foot - their thin shells make them especially sensitive to ocean acidification."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 2 ADDITIONS ==================================
{id:"c2-23", chapter:2, q:"The world's second-largest barrier reef system runs along a stretch of coastline shared by several countries in one region. Which region does it border?", options:["Northern Europe", "Eastern Africa", "Central America and the Caribbean", "The Arabian Peninsula"], correct:2, explain:"The Mesoamerican Barrier Reef runs along the coasts of Mexico, Belize, Guatemala and Honduras, and is the world's second-largest barrier reef system after the Great Barrier Reef."},
{id:"c2-24", chapter:2, q:"The process that carries carbon from the ocean's sunlit surface down into the deep sea, where it can be stored for long periods, is called the:", options:["The ocean engine", "The carbon conveyor", "The biological pump", "Marine circulation"], correct:2, explain:"The biological pump describes how phytoplankton absorb carbon dioxide near the surface; when they die or are eaten, some of that carbon sinks as marine snow into deep water, locking it away from the atmosphere for a long time."},
{id:"c2-25", chapter:2, q:"Which sequence best explains how carbon at the ocean surface can eventually become stored in the deep ocean for long periods?", options:["Carbon dioxide is absorbed by seawater, then immediately becomes permanently trapped in surface water", "Phytoplankton take up carbon dioxide, organic material is produced, some sinks as marine snow, and carbon is transported into deeper water", "Phytoplankton release carbon dioxide, which rises into the atmosphere and freezes into sea ice", "Carbon dioxide enters the ocean and sunlight converts it directly into rock that sinks to the seabed"], correct:1, explain:"This is the core mechanism of the biological pump: phytoplankton fix carbon through photosynthesis, and when some of that organic material sinks as marine snow, carbon is transported away from the surface into the deep ocean."},
{id:"c2-26", chapter:2, q:"Why does the ocean's role in storing carbon matter so much for the global climate, not just for marine life?", options:["It has no effect on climate at all", "By storing vast amounts of carbon that would otherwise stay in the atmosphere, the ocean slows the pace of global warming", "It only affects fish populations, not the atmosphere", "It causes all of the planet's warming"], correct:1, explain:"The ocean is one of the planet's largest carbon reservoirs; disrupting processes like the biological pump (for example through warming or acidification) could reduce the ocean's capacity to buffer climate change."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 3 ADDITIONS ==================================
{id:"c3-21", chapter:3, q:"Leatherback turtles feed heavily on jellyfish, but jellyfish can be difficult to keep hold of. Which adaptation helps leatherbacks prevent their soft-bodied prey from escaping?", options:["Backward-facing spines called papillae inside their mouth and throat", "Sharp teeth that slice through the jellyfish before swallowing", "Suction cups along their flippers", "A hard beak that crushes the jellyfish's outer body"], correct:0, explain:"Leatherback turtles have backward-pointing, spine-like papillae lining their mouth and oesophagus, which stop slippery jellyfish from sliding back out once swallowed."},
{id:"c3-22", chapter:3, q:"In 2021, which shark was identified as the largest known luminous (light-producing) vertebrate on Earth?", options:["Whale shark", "Kitefin shark", "Hammerhead shark", "Zebra shark"], correct:1, explain:"The kitefin shark, a deep-sea species, was confirmed in 2021 as the largest known bioluminescent vertebrate, glowing faintly to blend in with surface light when viewed from below."},
{id:"c3-23", chapter:3, q:"Certain large fish are heavily targeted by illegal fishers primarily for one internal organ, which is dried and sold at extremely high prices in some markets. Which organ is this?", options:["Scales", "Swim bladder", "Liver", "Teeth"], correct:1, explain:"Species like the totoaba are targeted almost entirely for their swim bladders (sometimes called 'fish maw'), which fetch very high prices in illegal wildlife trade - a trade that has also driven the vaquita porpoise, caught as bycatch in totoaba nets, to the edge of extinction."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 4 ADDITIONS ==================================
{id:"c4-21", chapter:4, q:"What is the term for the defensive behaviour in which a tightly packed school of fish suddenly scatters in all directions before quickly regrouping?", options:["Counterillumination", "Flash expansion (flash explosion)", "Bubble-netting", "Breaching"], correct:1, explain:"A flash expansion (sometimes called a 'flash explosion') momentarily confuses a predator by scattering the school outward in every direction; the fish then rapidly reform the group once the threat has passed."},
{id:"c4-22", chapter:4, q:"Long before satellite forecasts existed, Polynesian navigators crossed thousands of kilometres of open Pacific Ocean by reading natural cues, including the paths of certain animals returning to land at dusk. Which animals provided this particular clue?", options:["Dolphins", "Seabirds", "Sea turtles", "Whales"], correct:1, explain:"Traditional Polynesian wayfinders observed that many seabird species fly out to sea to feed during the day and return to roost on land each evening, so following their flight paths at dusk could help indicate the direction of the nearest land."},
{id:"c4-23", chapter:4, q:"Which sensory ability allows toothed whales (like dolphins and orcas) to build a detailed mental picture of nearby objects using reflected sound waves?", options:["Echolocation", "Chemoreception", "Electroreception", "Magnetoreception"], correct:0, explain:"By emitting clicks and interpreting the returning echoes, toothed whales can judge an object's distance, size, shape and even some internal structure - effectively 'seeing' with sound in dark or murky water."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 5 ADDITIONS ==================================
{id:"c5-18", chapter:5, q:"Minamata disease, one of the most famous pollution disasters, was caused by poisoning from which element?", options:["Mercury", "Arsenic", "Lead", "Cadmium"], correct:0, explain:"Minamata disease resulted from industrial wastewater releasing methylmercury into coastal waters, which then biomagnified up the food chain into fish eaten by local communities, causing severe neurological harm."},
{id:"c5-19", chapter:5, q:"Two coral reefs in the Indian Ocean experience the same rise in seawater temperature. However, scientists record different pH levels at each reef: Reef A: pH 7.8, Reef B: pH 8.1. Which reef would be expected to experience greater difficulty maintaining its calcium carbonate skeleton, and why?", options:["Reef B, because higher pH causes coral skeletons to dissolve more quickly", "Reef A, because lower pH means more acidic seawater, which makes it harder for corals to build and maintain their skeletons", "Reef A, because lower pH causes corals to grow too quickly for their skeletons to keep up", "Reef B, because higher pH prevents corals from absorbing nutrients from seawater"], correct:1, explain:"A lower pH value means more acidic water. More acidic seawater holds fewer of the carbonate ions corals need to build calcium carbonate skeletons, so Reef A (pH 7.8) would face greater difficulty than Reef B (pH 8.1)."},
{id:"c5-20", chapter:5, q:"Two reefs face a sudden spike in sea temperature. Reef A's spike lasts two weeks and the coral bounces back. Reef B's spike lasts nine weeks and the coral dies off. What most likely explains the difference?", options:["Reef B simply had more fish, which stressed the coral further", "Reef B had lower salt levels, which should have cooled the water", "Reef A was closer to the equator and naturally more heat-tolerant", "The marine heatwave at Reef B lasted long enough to exceed the corals' ability to recover"], correct:3, explain:"Corals can often survive brief heat stress by recovering once temperatures drop, but a prolonged marine heatwave gives them no chance to recover, making bleaching and death far more likely the longer the stressful period lasts."},
{id:"c5-21", chapter:5, q:"A reef gets hit by warm water, farm runoff, and heavy fishing, all in the same year. Its decline turns out far worse than any one of these alone would explain. What term fits this situation?", options:["A cumulative impact", "Biomagnification", "Ocean deoxygenation", "A trophic cascade"], correct:0, explain:"A cumulative impact occurs when multiple stressors act on an ecosystem at the same time, producing combined harm that is often worse than the sum of each individual stressor acting alone."},
{id:"c5-22", chapter:5, q:"An anti-plastic organisation realised that catching waste before it leaves rivers might work better than fishing it out of the open ocean later. What problem were they trying to get ahead of?", options:["How salty river water becomes near the coast", "How much plastic reaches the ocean from rivers in the first place", "How fast river currents flow into cities", "How quickly plastic breaks down once at sea"], correct:1, explain:"Rivers are a major pathway carrying land-based plastic waste out to sea, so intercepting it near the source, before it ever reaches the open ocean, can be far more efficient than trying to remove it once it has dispersed widely."},
{id:"c5-23", chapter:5, q:"A survey finds that India's waters host over 150 species of sharks and rays, yet roughly a quarter are threatened with extinction mostly because they are caught unintentionally in fisheries aimed at other species, rather than being deliberately targeted. What does this combination best illustrate about the conservation challenge here?", options:["Since most catches are unintentional, no conservation action is actually needed", "Species with high diversity are always safe from population decline", "High species diversity automatically means none of them can go extinct", "Conservation must address incidental capture across many different fisheries, not just a few deliberately-hunted species"], correct:3, explain:"When most harm comes from bycatch spread across numerous unrelated fisheries, effective conservation has to tackle incidental capture broadly (through gear changes, safe-release practices and monitoring), rather than focusing only on fisheries that specifically target sharks and rays."},

// ============================== CHAPTER 6 ADDITIONS ==================================
{id:"c6-19", chapter:6, q:"Which marine animal was the focus of India's first conservation reserve of its kind, established in Palk Bay, Tamil Nadu, in 2022?", options:["Dugong", "Whale shark", "Olive Ridley Turtle", "Blue Whale"], correct:0, explain:"The Dugong Conservation Reserve in Palk Bay, Tamil Nadu, established in 2022, was India's first conservation reserve dedicated specifically to protecting dugongs and their seagrass habitat."},
{id:"c6-20", chapter:6, q:"A vessel that turns off its tracking system while fishing is often attempting to avoid detection by which technology?", options:["AIS (Automatic Identification System)", "Radar reflector", "Sonar", "Echo sounder"], correct:0, explain:"AIS broadcasts a vessel's identity and location, helping authorities monitor fishing activity; deliberately switching it off ('going dark') is a common tactic used to mask illegal, unreported or unregulated (IUU) fishing."},
{id:"c6-21", chapter:6, q:"A country wants to designate specific zones for fishing, shipping lanes, wind farms, and conservation within its waters, to reduce conflict between these different uses. What is this planning process called?", options:["An environmental impact assessment", "The Blue Economy", "Ecosystem-based fisheries management", "Marine spatial planning"], correct:3, explain:"Marine spatial planning is the process of organising how and where different human activities take place in ocean areas, helping balance economic uses with conservation goals and reduce conflicts between them."},
{id:"c6-22", chapter:6, q:"Which fishery management approach adjusts regulations as new scientific information becomes available?", options:["Open-access management", "Adaptive management", "Fixed management", "Random management"], correct:1, explain:"Adaptive management treats regulations as flexible and open to revision, allowing managers to respond quickly as new data on stock health, climate impacts or ecosystem change comes in, rather than relying on fixed, unchanging rules."},
{id:"c6-23", chapter:6, q:"One fisheries agency sets catch limits based only on the target species' population. Another considers that species' predators, prey, and habitat too before setting limits. Which is more likely to avoid unexpected side effects elsewhere in the ecosystem?", options:["The agency considering predators, prey, and habitat", "Neither approach affects ecosystem outcomes", "Both approaches produce results in negative outcomes", "The agency focused only on the target species"], correct:0, explain:"This reflects the core idea of ecosystem-based management: considering a target species in the context of its predators, prey and habitat helps managers anticipate and avoid knock-on effects elsewhere in the food web."},
{id:"c6-24", chapter:6, q:"An international scientific collaboration spanning roughly a decade brought together thousands of researchers from more than 80 countries with one overarching aim: producing the first comprehensive assessment of what actually lives in the world's oceans. What was this effort called?", options:["The Census of Marine Life", "The Argo Program", "The Global Ghost Gear Initiative", "The UN Decade of Ocean Science"], correct:0, explain:"The Census of Marine Life (2000-2010) was a landmark international research program that catalogued marine biodiversity worldwide, discovering thousands of previously unknown species and creating a baseline for future ocean research."},
{id:"c6-25", chapter:6, q:"Watch the video of a floating barrier being towed slowly through the Great Pacific Garbage Patch by two vessels. Based on what you see, what is the barrier mainly doing as it moves?", options:["Dropping a net from an aircraft overhead", "Spraying a chemical that dissolves plastic on contact", "Picking up each piece of plastic individually with robotic arms", "Funnelling plastic into a retention zone as it's towed along"], correct:3, explain:"Large-scale ocean cleanup systems work by slowly towing a U-shaped floating barrier through the water, which funnels floating plastic debris toward a retention zone at the back, where it accumulates and can later be removed."},
{id:"c6-26", chapter:6, q:"A research team wants to monitor long-term temperature and salinity changes across an entire ocean basin without deploying research vessels. Which of the following tools is specifically built for this large-scale, long-term task?", options:["A global network of autonomous Argo floats", "Remotely Operated Vehicles (ROVs)", "A local eDNA sample", "Satellite-tagged shark"], correct:0, explain:"The Argo program consists of thousands of autonomous floats drifting across the world's oceans, regularly diving and resurfacing to measure temperature and salinity, and transmitting the data by satellite - enabling continuous, basin-wide monitoring without research ships."},

// ============================== NEW CHAPTER 7 - Global Ocean Governance, Law & Wildlife Trade ==================================
{id:"c7-01", chapter:7, q:"The OSPAR Convention focuses on protecting the marine environment of which region?", options:["Coral Triangle", "North-East Atlantic", "Red Sea", "Southern Ocean"], correct:1, explain:"The OSPAR Convention is a regional agreement between governments that protects the marine environment of the North-East Atlantic, covering issues like pollution and biodiversity loss."},
{id:"c7-02", chapter:7, q:"Which convention focuses on protecting the marine environment of the Mediterranean Sea?", options:["BBNJ Agreement", "OSPAR Convention", "Ramsar Convention", "Barcelona Convention"], correct:3, explain:"The Barcelona Convention, adopted in 1976, is a regional agreement among Mediterranean countries to protect the marine and coastal environment of that sea."},
{id:"c7-03", chapter:7, q:"The Ramsar Convention is an international treaty primarily focused on the conservation of:", options:["Wetlands, including many coastal and marine wetland habitats", "Deep-sea mining sites", "Shipping lanes", "Migratory birds only, with no connection to water bodies"], correct:0, explain:"The Ramsar Convention (1971) is dedicated to the conservation of wetlands of international importance, a category that includes many coastal mangroves, estuaries and mudflats vital to marine life."},
{id:"c7-04", chapter:7, q:"Adopted in 2023, which treaty is specifically designed to protect biodiversity in areas of the ocean beyond any single country's national jurisdiction (the High Seas)?", options:["The BBNJ Agreement (High Seas Treaty)", "UNCLOS", "The Paris Agreement", "MARPOL"], correct:0, explain:"The BBNJ Agreement (Biodiversity Beyond National Jurisdiction), agreed in 2023, fills a major gap by creating tools - like the ability to establish marine protected areas - for conserving biodiversity on the High Seas."},
{id:"c7-05", chapter:7, q:"CITES is an international agreement mainly concerned with:", options:["Regulating international trade in endangered species, including many sharks and rays", "Setting speed limits for cargo ships", "Measuring sea surface temperature", "Funding coral restoration projects only"], correct:0, explain:"CITES (the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) regulates and monitors cross-border trade in species at risk, and has increasingly added shark, ray and other marine species to its protected listings."},
{id:"c7-06", chapter:7, q:"Which international agreement specifically targets pollution from ships, including oil discharges and garbage dumped at sea?", options:["MARPOL", "CBD", "Ramsar Convention", "CITES"], correct:0, explain:"MARPOL (the International Convention for the Prevention of Pollution from Ships) sets binding rules to reduce operational and accidental pollution from vessels, including oil, chemicals, sewage and garbage."},
{id:"c7-07", chapter:7, q:"What is a Regional Fisheries Management Organisation (RFMO)?", options:["A body through which multiple countries cooperate to manage shared fish stocks, such as migratory tuna, across a wider ocean region", "A single country's national fisheries department", "An organisation that only studies coral reefs", "A private seafood company"], correct:0, explain:"Because many commercially important species like tuna migrate across national boundaries and the High Seas, RFMOs bring countries together to set shared catch limits and rules that no single nation could enforce alone."},
{id:"c7-08", chapter:7, q:"The term 'Blue Economy' refers to:", options:["Sustainable use of ocean resources for economic growth, improved livelihoods, and jobs, while preserving ocean ecosystem health", "Any economic activity that takes place near a blue-coloured sea", "A tax placed on seafood imports", "A term for illegal fishing profits"], correct:0, explain:"The Blue Economy concept tries to balance using ocean resources (fisheries, tourism, shipping, renewable energy) for development with maintaining the long-term health of marine ecosystems."},
{id:"c7-09", chapter:7, q:"Under international maritime law, which country generally holds primary responsibility for ensuring a ship complies with environmental and safety regulations, regardless of where it is sailing?", options:["The country whose port it most recently departed from", "Its 'flag state' - the country under whose flag the vessel is registered", "Whichever country owns the nearest coastline", "The United Nations directly"], correct:1, explain:"Flag state responsibility is a cornerstone of maritime law: the country where a vessel is registered is primarily responsible for enforcing safety, labour and environmental standards on board, which can be a challenge when ships register under countries with weak enforcement ('flags of convenience')."},
{id:"c7-10", chapter:7, q:"The 'precautionary principle', often applied in ocean conservation and fisheries policy, means that:", options:["Action to prevent environmental harm should not be delayed just because of full scientific uncertainty", "No action should ever be taken without 100% scientific certainty", "Fishing should always be banned everywhere", "Only wealthy countries need to follow conservation rules"], correct:0, explain:"The precautionary principle supports taking protective action - such as setting cautious catch limits - even when scientific understanding of a risk is incomplete, rather than waiting for certainty that may come too late."},
{id:"c7-11", chapter:7, q:"The Convention on Migratory Species (CMS) specifically aims to protect:", options:["Only animals that stay permanently within one country's borders", "Species like sea turtles, whales and sharks that regularly cross national boundaries during their life cycle", "Only freshwater fish", "Coral reefs that do not move at all"], correct:1, explain:"Because migratory marine species can cross many countries' waters and the High Seas during their journeys, CMS encourages range-state countries to cooperate on protecting them throughout their migratory routes."},
{id:"c7-12", chapter:7, q:"A 'UNESCO World Heritage Marine Site' is a designation given to:", options:["Any beach popular with tourists", "Marine areas recognised for outstanding universal natural or cultural value, such as the Great Barrier Reef", "A site reserved exclusively for naval exercises", "A region banned from all scientific research"], correct:1, explain:"World Heritage Marine Sites are chosen for their exceptional natural significance and receive international recognition intended to support their long-term protection."},
{id:"c7-13", chapter:7, q:"UN Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 14, 'Life Below Water', commits countries to:", options:["Eliminating poverty on land", "Conserving and sustainably using the oceans, seas and marine resources", "Building more commercial fishing fleets", "Reducing the number of marine protected areas"], correct:1, explain:"SDG 14 is one of 17 global goals adopted by UN member states, specifically targeting the health of oceans, including reducing pollution, protecting ecosystems, and ending overfishing and IUU fishing."},
{id:"c7-14", chapter:7, q:"A 'Regional Seas Programme', coordinated in many cases by the UN Environment Programme, is designed to:", options:["Bring neighbouring countries together to jointly address shared marine environmental challenges in a particular sea region", "Replace the need for any national environmental laws", "Only apply to Arctic waters", "Fund deep-sea mining exploration"], correct:0, explain:"Regional Seas Programmes (such as those behind the Barcelona and OSPAR Conventions) help neighbouring coastal states coordinate on shared challenges like pollution and habitat loss, since marine ecosystems don't respect political borders."},
{id:"c7-15", chapter:7, q:"Why are international wildlife trade agreements like CITES especially important for species such as sawfish, seahorses, and sea cucumbers?", options:["Because these species are not actually affected by trade at all", "Because high demand in international markets can drive intense, often illegal, harvesting that depletes wild populations faster than they can recover", "Because these species can only be traded within a single country", "Because CITES only applies to land animals"], correct:1, explain:"Strong market demand - for products like sawfish rostra, dried seahorses, or sea cucumbers - has driven serious population declines in multiple species, making coordinated international trade controls an important conservation tool."},
{id:"c7-16", chapter:7, q:"'Port State Measures' refer to steps taken by:", options:["Fishing vessels themselves, to self-regulate while at sea", "Countries where foreign fishing vessels dock, to inspect and deny port access to vessels suspected of illegal fishing", "Only countries without any coastline", "International shipping insurance companies"], correct:1, explain:"Under the Port State Measures Agreement, countries can inspect foreign-flagged fishing vessels seeking to use their ports, and deny access or services to vessels suspected of involvement in illegal, unreported or unregulated (IUU) fishing - closing off a key route by which illegally caught fish reach markets."},
{id:"c7-17", chapter:7, q:"Community-based co-management of a fishery typically involves:", options:["Local fishing communities and government authorities sharing responsibility for making and enforcing management decisions", "Removing all local community involvement from decision-making", "Only scientists making decisions, with no community input", "A total ban on any form of fishing"], correct:0, explain:"Co-management combines local ecological knowledge and community buy-in with government oversight, often leading to rules that are both more locally appropriate and more effectively followed."},
{id:"c7-18", chapter:7, q:"Which of these best describes why cooperation across many countries is especially necessary to protect species on the High Seas?", options:["Because the High Seas belong entirely to one country", "Because no single nation has jurisdiction over the High Seas, so protection depends on multilateral agreement and action", "Because the High Seas contain no biodiversity worth protecting", "Because High Seas species never migrate"], correct:1, explain:"Since the High Seas lie beyond any country's Exclusive Economic Zone, effective protection of the species and ecosystems there requires international cooperation - which is exactly the gap the 2023 BBNJ Agreement was designed to help close."},
{id:"c7-19", chapter:7, q:"Fisheries subsidies - government financial support to fishing industries - have drawn international attention (including a 2022 WTO agreement) mainly because some subsidies:", options:["Always help conserve fish stocks", "Can encourage excess fishing capacity and contribute to overfishing, including on the High Seas", "Have no economic effect on fishing fleets", "Apply only to recreational fishing"], correct:1, explain:"Certain subsidies (for example for fuel or vessel construction) can make unsustainable, excess fishing effort artificially profitable, which is why reducing harmful fisheries subsidies became the focus of a landmark 2022 WTO agreement."},
{id:"c7-20", chapter:7, q:"Why is it significant that an international agreement specifically allows for the creation of Marine Protected Areas on the High Seas?", options:["Because previously, there was no clear legal mechanism to protect biodiversity in waters beyond any country's national jurisdiction", "Because High Seas MPAs were already common before this agreement", "Because it bans all countries from having their own MPAs", "Because it applies only to Arctic waters"], correct:0, explain:"Before agreements like the BBNJ Treaty, there was no established global legal process for designating protected areas in the roughly two-thirds of the ocean that lies beyond national jurisdiction, leaving these biodiversity-rich waters largely unprotected."},

// ============================== NEW CHAPTER 8 - Ocean Monitoring Technology & Science ==================================
{id:"c8-01", chapter:8, q:"Satellite altimetry, used to track long-term changes in global sea level, works by:", options:["Measuring the precise height of the ocean surface from space using radar", "Lowering physical rulers into the ocean from ships", "Counting the number of waves per minute", "Measuring air temperature only"], correct:0, explain:"Satellite altimeters bounce radar signals off the sea surface and measure the time taken for the signal to return, allowing scientists to track sea-level changes across the entire globe over decades."},
{id:"c8-02", chapter:8, q:"Pop-up satellite archival tags attached to animals like sharks and tuna are especially useful because they:", options:["Require the animal to be recaptured before any data can be recovered", "Detach after a set period, float to the surface, and transmit stored movement and depth data via satellite - without needing the animal to be caught again", "Only work on animals living in captivity", "Can only record sound, not location"], correct:1, explain:"Because these tags release automatically and beam data to satellites once they surface, scientists can track long migrations of wide-ranging ocean animals without ever needing to physically recapture them."},
{id:"c8-03", chapter:8, q:"Multibeam sonar is primarily used by ocean scientists to:", options:["Create detailed 3D maps of the seafloor's depth and shape", "Translate dolphin communication into human language", "Measure the salt content of seawater", "Detect carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere"], correct:0, explain:"By sending out and timing many sound beams at once, multibeam sonar builds detailed bathymetric (seafloor depth) maps, revealing underwater mountains, trenches and habitats that would otherwise be invisible."},
{id:"c8-04", chapter:8, q:"Satellite sensors that detect chlorophyll concentration in surface waters allow scientists to remotely monitor:", options:["Phytoplankton abundance and the location of productive blooms across huge ocean areas", "The exact species of fish present", "Underwater noise levels", "Shipwreck locations"], correct:0, explain:"Because phytoplankton contain chlorophyll, satellite ocean-colour sensors can estimate their abundance across vast areas, helping track productivity, upwelling zones and harmful algal blooms without needing to sample the water directly."},
{id:"c8-05", chapter:8, q:"Underwater gliders used in ocean research are valuable because they:", options:["Can travel for weeks or months collecting data while using very little energy, by changing their buoyancy rather than using propellers", "Must return to the surface every few minutes", "Can only operate in swimming pools", "Require a research vessel to tow them constantly"], correct:0, explain:"Gliders move by subtly adjusting their buoyancy to glide up and down through the water in a sawtooth pattern, letting them cover long distances and long durations on minimal power compared to propeller-driven vehicles."},
{id:"c8-06", chapter:8, q:"What is the key difference between eDNA sampling and DNA barcoding as used in marine science?", options:["eDNA detects species from genetic traces in the environment (like water), while DNA barcoding identifies a species from a genetic sample taken directly from a specimen", "They are exactly the same technique", "DNA barcoding can only be used on plants", "eDNA requires capturing the whole animal first"], correct:0, explain:"eDNA analysis detects species presence from traces like shed cells or mucus in a water sample, while DNA barcoding typically uses a short genetic sequence from a known tissue sample to confirm or identify a species."},
{id:"c8-07", chapter:8, q:"Acoustic telemetry, used to track the movements of tagged fish or sharks near coastlines, relies on:", options:["Tagged animals emitting an acoustic signal that is detected by a network of fixed underwater receivers as the animal swims past", "Satellites photographing the animal from space", "Measuring the fish's body temperature only", "The animal returning to the same tagging location every day"], correct:0, explain:"Acoustic tags emit a unique 'ping' detected by receivers moored at fixed locations, allowing researchers to build up a detailed picture of an animal's local movements and habitat use over time."},
{id:"c8-08", chapter:8, q:"Drone (aerial) surveys are increasingly used in marine conservation to:", options:["Count whales, turtles or dugongs and monitor beach nesting activity from above without disturbing the animals", "Replace the need for any ocean conservation policy", "Only measure wind speed", "Communicate directly with fish"], correct:0, explain:"Drones can cover large stretches of coastline or open water quickly and non-invasively, making them useful for counting animals, monitoring nesting beaches, and detecting illegal fishing activity."},
{id:"c8-09", chapter:8, q:"BRUVS (Baited Remote Underwater Video Systems) are used by researchers to:", options:["Attract and film marine animals, including sharks, without divers needing to enter the water", "Only record sounds, not images", "Clean plastic from the seafloor", "Tag animals with GPS trackers automatically"], correct:0, explain:"BRUVS use bait to attract animals into camera view, providing a standardised, diver-independent way to survey species abundance and behaviour, including in deep or remote locations."},
{id:"c8-10", chapter:8, q:"Stable isotope analysis of animal tissue is a technique scientists use mainly to study:", options:["An animal's diet and position in the food web", "The animal's exact age in days", "The colour of the animal's skin", "The direction an animal is currently swimming"], correct:0, explain:"Because the chemical signatures of certain isotopes change in predictable ways as they pass through a food web, analysing them in tissue samples helps scientists reconstruct what an animal has been eating and its trophic position."},
{id:"c8-11", chapter:8, q:"Counting growth rings in a fish's otolith (ear bone) is a technique used to determine:", options:["The fish's age", "The fish's exact location of birth", "The fish's genetic relatives", "The water temperature where the fish currently lives"], correct:0, explain:"Much like tree rings, otoliths form layered growth rings that can be counted to estimate a fish's age, which is essential information for sustainable fisheries management."},
{id:"c8-12", chapter:8, q:"Genetic stock identification allows fisheries scientists to determine:", options:["Which distinct population or 'stock' a caught fish most likely belongs to, even if different stocks look identical", "The exact weight of a fish before it is caught", "Whether a fish is male or female using sound alone", "The migratory route of plankton"], correct:0, explain:"Different populations of the same species can have subtly different genetic markers; identifying these helps managers set catch limits appropriate to each distinct stock, rather than treating a whole species as one uniform population."},
{id:"c8-13", chapter:8, q:"Long-term ocean monitoring stations that repeatedly sample the same location over decades are valuable mainly because they:", options:["Provide a continuous time-series that reveals slow, long-term trends - such as warming, acidification or deoxygenation - that a single survey could never detect", "Only work for one single year of data collection", "Replace the need for any satellite data", "Can only monitor coral reefs"], correct:0, explain:"Because gradual ocean changes unfold over years to decades, long-running monitoring stations are essential for distinguishing genuine long-term trends from natural short-term variability."},
{id:"c8-14", chapter:8, q:"What is the key difference between AIS and VMS (Vessel Monitoring System) in fisheries monitoring?", options:["They are identical systems with different names", "AIS is broadcast openly and can be picked up by many receivers, while VMS typically reports a vessel's position confidentially to fisheries authorities via satellite", "VMS only works on land", "AIS can only be used by naval ships"], correct:1, explain:"AIS signals are broadcast openly for collision avoidance and can be received by any properly equipped receiver (including satellites), whereas VMS data is usually transmitted more securely and directly to a country's fisheries management authority."},
{id:"c8-15", chapter:8, q:"Citizen science smartphone apps that let members of the public photograph and log marine species sightings contribute to conservation mainly by:", options:["Replacing all professional scientific research", "Generating large volumes of observational data across huge geographic areas that would be impossible for scientists to collect alone", "Only being useful for identifying plastic waste", "Automatically enforcing fishing bans"], correct:1, explain:"Crowdsourced sightings can reveal species distributions, range shifts and bleaching events across far more locations and more often than professional survey teams could manage on their own."},
{id:"c8-16", chapter:8, q:"Thermal imaging cameras are sometimes used in marine wildlife surveys because they can:", options:["Detect the heat signatures of animals such as surfacing whales or dugongs, including at night or from a distance", "Only work underwater", "Measure the pH of seawater directly", "Replace the need for any animal tagging"], correct:0, explain:"Thermal cameras can pick up the warmth of an animal's body or breath against the cooler background of water or air, helping researchers detect and count animals even in low-visibility or night-time conditions."},
{id:"c8-17", chapter:8, q:"A global network of moored and drifting ocean buoys that continuously relay data on waves, temperature, and currents back to shore stations supports which broader goal?", options:["Eliminating the need for any future ocean research", "Providing real-time ocean observations that feed into weather forecasting, climate models and early warning systems", "Replacing fishing vessels entirely", "Mapping the surface of the Moon"], correct:1, explain:"Continuous buoy networks form part of the broader Global Ocean Observing System, supplying real-time data that underpins weather prediction, climate monitoring, and hazard warnings such as for tsunamis."},
{id:"c8-18", chapter:8, q:"Why do scientists often combine several different technologies - such as satellite tagging, eDNA, and acoustic telemetry - rather than relying on just one method to study a marine species?", options:["Because using only one method is always illegal", "Because each technology reveals different kinds of information, and combining them gives a more complete picture of a species' biology and movements", "Because combining technologies is required to reduce costs", "Because a single technology always gives contradictory results"], correct:1, explain:"Different tools answer different questions - for instance tagging reveals long-distance movement, eDNA reveals local presence, and acoustic telemetry reveals fine-scale habitat use - so combining methods builds a much fuller understanding than any single approach alone."},

// ============================== NEW CHAPTER 9 - Applied Reasoning: Comparing Scenarios (Round 2 style) ==================================
{id:"c9-01", chapter:9, q:"Species A has a narrow habitat range restricted to a single reef system. Species B has a wide range spanning multiple oceans. All else being equal, which species is generally more vulnerable to a localised disaster such as a single oil spill?", options:["Species A, because its entire population could be affected by damage to that one location", "Species B, because wide-ranging species are always more vulnerable", "Neither is more vulnerable, since range has no effect on vulnerability", "Species A, because narrow-range species always reproduce more slowly"], correct:0, explain:"A species confined to one location has nowhere to retreat to if that specific habitat is damaged, while a widely distributed species is more likely to have unaffected populations elsewhere that can help it persist."},
{id:"c9-02", chapter:9, q:"Population X reproduces slowly, matures late, and has few offspring (like many sharks). Population Y reproduces quickly, matures early, and has many offspring (like many small bony fish). After both suffer the same percentage decline from overfishing, which is likely to recover faster?", options:["Population Y, because high reproductive rates allow faster rebuilding of numbers", "Population X, because slow-growing species always recover faster", "Both recover at exactly the same rate regardless of biology", "Neither population can ever recover once declined"], correct:0, explain:"Species with fast reproduction and early maturity, like many small bony fish, can rebuild their numbers more quickly after a decline than slow-reproducing species like sharks, which take much longer to replace lost individuals."},
{id:"c9-03", chapter:9, q:"Country A enforces its Marine Protected Area with regular patrols and penalties for illegal fishing. Country B has designated an MPA on paper but has no enforcement budget. Which MPA is more likely to deliver real conservation benefits?", options:["Country B's, because any designation automatically protects an area", "Country A's, because active enforcement is necessary to actually prevent harmful activities within the protected area", "Neither MPA can ever provide any benefit", "Both are equally effective regardless of enforcement"], correct:1, explain:"Research consistently shows that 'paper parks' - MPAs that exist in name but lack enforcement - often fail to deliver meaningful conservation outcomes, while actively enforced MPAs can produce measurable increases in fish abundance and biodiversity."},
{id:"c9-04", chapter:9, q:"A fishery sets its catch limit using last year's population estimate, updated only once every five years. Another fishery updates its catch limit every season based on the latest stock surveys. Which approach is better suited to a stock facing rapid climate-driven change?", options:["The five-year approach, because stability is always better than flexibility", "The seasonal approach, because frequent updates let managers respond quickly to real-time changes in the stock", "Neither approach matters if the species is not currently overfished", "The five-year approach, because frequent changes confuse fish populations"], correct:1, explain:"When ocean conditions and stock sizes are shifting quickly due to factors like warming or changing currents, an adaptive, frequently-updated approach can respond to new information much faster than a rarely-revised one, reducing the risk of setting limits that are already outdated."},
{id:"c9-05", chapter:9, q:"Two coastal towns both discharge untreated wastewater. Town A's waters have strong tidal currents that disperse pollutants quickly. Town B's waters are in a nearly enclosed, low-circulation bay. Where would pollutant concentrations likely build up to more harmful levels?", options:["Town A, because currents always concentrate pollution", "Town B, because limited water circulation allows pollutants to accumulate rather than disperse", "Neither location would show any difference", "Town A, because tides remove all oxygen from the water"], correct:1, explain:"In an enclosed, low-circulation bay, pollutants are not flushed out efficiently, so they can accumulate to much higher, more damaging concentrations than in a well-mixed, high-circulation area."},
{id:"c9-06", chapter:9, q:"Reef A has high fish biomass and many large predatory fish. Reef B has very low fish biomass and few large predators, due to decades of heavy fishing. Based on general reef ecology, which reef is more likely to also show healthier coral cover and less algal overgrowth?", options:["Reef B, because fewer predators always means healthier coral", "Reef A, because a fuller food web (including herbivorous fish kept in balance by predators) tends to support better control of algae that can otherwise overwhelm coral", "Neither factor is connected to coral health in any way", "Reef B, because low fish numbers reduce competition for coral space"], correct:1, explain:"Healthy populations of herbivorous fish (which graze on algae) and the predators that help regulate the broader food web are linked to reefs that better resist algal overgrowth, helping coral maintain space and light to grow."},
{id:"c9-07", chapter:9, q:"Two island nations both experience the same amount of sea-level rise. Nation A has extensive mangrove forests along its coast. Nation B has cleared most of its mangroves for development. Which nation is likely to experience less coastal erosion and storm damage?", options:["Nation B, because cleared coastlines are always more stable", "Nation A, because mangrove roots stabilise sediment and buffer wave energy, reducing erosion and storm impact", "Neither nation is affected differently by mangrove cover", "Nation B, because mangroves increase storm intensity"], correct:1, explain:"Mangrove root systems trap sediment and absorb wave energy, acting as a natural buffer; nations that retain their mangroves generally experience less severe coastal erosion and storm surge damage than those that have removed them."},
{id:"c9-08", chapter:9, q:"A marine heatwave hits two populations of the same coral species. Population A has previously experienced repeated, moderate heat stress and shows signs of resistance. Population B has never previously experienced any heat stress. Which population is more likely to show greater resilience during this new heatwave?", options:["Population B, because no prior history is always better", "Population A, because prior exposure can allow some corals to develop greater tolerance to subsequent heat stress", "Neither has any likely difference in resilience", "Population A, because any heat exposure always weakens corals permanently"], correct:1, explain:"Some coral populations exposed to repeated, non-lethal heat stress can develop increased thermal tolerance (sometimes linked to changes in their algal symbionts), making them more likely to withstand a subsequent heatwave compared to populations with no such history."},
{id:"c9-09", chapter:9, q:"Two fishing fleets both operate in the same waters. Fleet A uses circle hooks and is required to release sea turtles safely if caught. Fleet B uses traditional J-hooks with no release protocol. Which fleet is likely to cause less sea turtle bycatch mortality?", options:["Fleet B, because hook shape makes no real difference", "Fleet A, because circle hooks reduce the chance of deep hooking and safe-release protocols improve survival of accidentally caught turtles", "Neither fleet's practices affect turtle survival", "Fleet B, because J-hooks are always safer for turtles"], correct:1, explain:"Circle hooks are less likely to be swallowed deeply than J-hooks, causing less internal injury, and combined with safe-handling and release protocols, this combination significantly reduces sea turtle bycatch mortality."},
{id:"c9-10", chapter:9, q:"A government wants to reduce plastic pollution reaching the ocean. Option A: fund clean-up vessels to remove plastic already floating in the open ocean. Option B: invest in waste management infrastructure to stop plastic entering rivers in the first place. Which option generally addresses the problem more efficiently, according to conservation research?", options:["Option A, because open-ocean clean-up is always cheaper and faster", "Option B, because preventing plastic from entering waterways addresses the problem at its source, before it disperses across vast and hard-to-reach ocean areas", "Neither option has any effect on ocean plastic levels", "Option A, because waste infrastructure has no connection to ocean pollution"], correct:1, explain:"Once plastic disperses across the open ocean it becomes extremely difficult and costly to collect; intercepting it at the source - rivers and coastal waste systems - is generally considered a far more efficient use of resources."},
{id:"c9-11", chapter:9, q:"Two deep-sea mining proposals are compared. Site A would disturb a hydrothermal vent ecosystem with many endemic species found nowhere else. Site B would disturb a seafloor area with widely distributed, common species. Which site poses a greater extinction risk if mining proceeds?", options:["Site B, because common species are always more fragile", "Site A, because disturbing a habitat with endemic species found only there could drive species extinct if their sole habitat is destroyed", "Neither site poses any extinction risk", "Site A, because endemic species are immune to habitat disturbance"], correct:1, explain:"Endemic species, by definition, exist nowhere else - so destroying their only known habitat (as could happen at a unique vent ecosystem) carries a much higher extinction risk than disturbing habitat used by widely distributed species that persist elsewhere."},
{id:"c9-12", chapter:9, q:"One coastal community relies on traditional ecological knowledge passed down over generations to decide when to harvest shellfish. Another relies solely on a fixed calendar date each year regardless of conditions. Which approach is more likely to avoid harvesting during a toxic algal bloom?", options:["The fixed calendar approach, because dates are always accurate", "The traditional knowledge approach, if it incorporates observation of current water and organism conditions rather than a rigid schedule", "Neither approach can ever detect a toxic bloom", "Both approaches are identical in effectiveness"], correct:1, explain:"Traditional ecological knowledge often includes close, ongoing observation of local conditions (such as water colour or shellfish behaviour), which can help communities avoid harvesting during unsafe conditions like a bloom - something a fixed calendar date cannot account for."},
{id:"c9-13", chapter:9, q:"Two whale populations are both recovering from historical whaling. Population A is protected across its entire migratory range by multiple cooperating countries. Population B is protected in only one country's waters, with no protection in the rest of its range. Which population faces a greater ongoing threat?", options:["Population A, because cooperation between countries always increases threats", "Population B, because gaps in protection across the rest of its migratory range leave it vulnerable outside the one protected country", "Neither population faces any different level of threat", "Population B, because partial protection is always sufficient"], correct:1, explain:"Migratory species can spend significant time outside any single country's protected waters; if protection is not coordinated across the whole migratory range, the population remains vulnerable during the unprotected portions of its journey."},
{id:"c9-14", chapter:9, q:"Two aquaculture (fish farming) operations are compared. Farm A is sited in an open bay with strong currents and uses low stocking densities. Farm B is sited in an enclosed, low-circulation inlet with high stocking densities. Which farm is more likely to cause local water quality problems from waste build-up?", options:["Farm A, because currents always concentrate waste", "Farm B, because high stocking density combined with poor water circulation allows waste and nutrients to accumulate locally", "Neither farm's siting or stocking density affects water quality", "Farm A, because open bays trap more waste than enclosed inlets"], correct:1, explain:"High stocking density produces more waste and uneaten feed, and in a low-circulation inlet this waste cannot disperse efficiently, increasing the risk of nutrient pollution, oxygen depletion and harmful algal blooms around the farm."},
{id:"c9-15", chapter:9, q:"A scientist is comparing two methods for estimating a shark population's size. Method A involves one single survey dive. Method B involves repeated surveys across several seasons, tagging and acoustic tracking combined. Which method generally produces a more reliable estimate of true population trends?", options:["Method A, since a single snapshot is always sufficient", "Method B, because combining repeated observations over time with tagging data accounts for seasonal movement and natural variability that a single survey would miss", "Neither method is more reliable than the other", "Method A, because more data collection always introduces more error"], correct:1, explain:"Shark populations can vary seasonally and individuals may move in and out of a survey area; combining repeated surveys with tagging and tracking data over time gives a far more robust picture of real population trends than a single snapshot survey."},
{id:"c9-16", chapter:9, q:"Two reef fish species are compared. Species A can only survive within a narrow temperature range. Species B can tolerate a wide range of temperatures. As ocean temperatures become more variable due to climate change, which species faces a greater risk of local decline?", options:["Species B, because tolerance is always a disadvantage", "Species A, because a narrow thermal tolerance leaves less margin for survival as temperatures swing further from its preferred range", "Neither species is affected by temperature variability", "Species A, because narrow tolerance always means faster reproduction"], correct:1, explain:"Species with a narrow thermal tolerance have less flexibility to cope with temperature swings; as climate change increases variability and extremes, such species are generally at greater risk of local population decline than more thermally tolerant species."},
{id:"c9-17", chapter:9, q:"Two fisheries both catch the same target species. Fishery A uses selective gear that lets juvenile fish escape before capture. Fishery B uses gear that catches fish of all sizes indiscriminately. Which fishery is more likely to remain productive over the long term?", options:["Fishery B, because catching all sizes immediately maximises long-term yield", "Fishery A, because allowing juveniles to escape and mature lets them reproduce first, helping sustain the population that supplies future catches", "Neither fishery's gear selectivity affects long-term productivity", "Fishery B, because juvenile fish contribute nothing to future populations"], correct:1, explain:"Allowing juvenile fish to escape and reach reproductive age helps maintain the breeding stock that replenishes the population, supporting more sustainable catches over the long run compared to indiscriminately catching fish of all sizes."},
{id:"c9-18", chapter:9, q:"A coastal wetland is compared under two scenarios. Scenario A: the wetland remains intact and connected to the sea. Scenario B: a road is built across it, cutting off most tidal water flow. Under which scenario would the wetland better continue supporting juvenile fish nurseries?", options:["Scenario B, because roads improve access for fish", "Scenario A, because maintaining tidal connectivity preserves the flow of water, nutrients and juvenile fish between the wetland and the sea", "Neither scenario affects the wetland's nursery function", "Scenario B, because cutting off tidal flow increases nursery habitat"], correct:1, explain:"Many coastal wetlands function as nurseries because tidal flow connects them to the sea, allowing young fish to move in to feed and shelter before returning to open water; blocking this flow (for example with a road or dyke) typically degrades their nursery value."},
{id:"c9-19", chapter:9, q:"Two countries both rely heavily on coral reef tourism. Country A invests in reef restoration and visitor number limits. Country B allows unlimited tourist access with no restoration efforts. Over several decades, which country's reefs are more likely to continue supporting their tourism industry?", options:["Country A, because managing visitor pressure and actively restoring reefs helps maintain the ecosystem that the tourism industry itself depends on", "Country B, because unlimited access always benefits reefs", "Neither country's management choices affect long-term reef health", "Country B, because restoration efforts always harm natural reef recovery"], correct:0, explain:"An ecosystem that tourism depends on must itself be maintained to keep supporting that industry; limiting visitor pressure and investing in restoration helps sustain reef health, while unlimited, unmanaged access tends to degrade the very asset the industry relies on."},
{id:"c9-20", chapter:9, q:"Two research teams both want to assess whether a newly declared fishing closure is working. Team A compares fish counts only from inside the closure, one year before versus one year after. Team B compares fish counts inside the closure AND in similar nearby unprotected areas, both before and after. Which approach better isolates the effect of the closure itself?", options:["Team A's, because comparing only one location is always sufficient", "Team B's, because comparing protected and unprotected areas over the same period helps separate the closure's effect from other region-wide changes (like a natural good year for fish)", "Neither approach can reveal anything about the closure's effectiveness", "Team A's, because adding more comparison sites always adds confusion"], correct:1, explain:"Without a comparison ('control') area, it's hard to know whether a change inside the closure was actually caused by the closure or by some broader regional factor (like weather or currents) that would have affected fish numbers everywhere. Comparing protected versus similar unprotected sites over the same time period helps isolate the closure's true effect."},
];

